  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\n- Dry through Saturday with gradually increasing temperatures\n\nForecast remains unchanged through Saturday, with dry weather \nexpected and increasingly warmer temperatures.\n\nConfluent flow aloft downstream of the upper ridge over the Plains \nwill dominate the weather through Friday. This scenario provides for \na strong sfc ridge axis remaining over the area through Friday also. \n\nOne small interesting feature is the Euro and HRRR keep showing a \nfew sprinkles this morning near the lakeshore. This looks to be the \nresult of of a land breeze convergent band over the lake, with the \nwarmer lake contributing some moisture. This should not be anything \nmore that sprinkles, and surely not impactful.\n\nWe will see the sfc ridge axis weaken and sink south of the area on \nSaturday. This will allow for an increase in the low level flow from \nthe SW. All indications are that there is not much moisture yet \nadvecting in, and no feature yet to trigger anything through the \ndaylight hours.\n\n- Multiple chances for storms Saturday night through Monday\n\nThe models and their ensemble members have been fairly consistent in \nbringing the first chances of rain to the area Saturday night. We \nsee better moisture able to start advecting in, and we have a short \nwave riding north of the area to help bring some forcing to interact \nwith increasing instability. There is not shown to be much of a low \nlevel jet, which would limit rain chances.\n\nRain chances look to be better Sunday night and early Monday morning \nin general across the region. There is good agreement that we see a \nbetter organized low level jet expected to move over the area, along \nwith plenty of instability in place. The better chances for rain are \nlikely to be over Northern Michigan and Canada where the nose of the \nlow level jet will be. We shall see though where it ends up, as it \ncould propagate a little further south.\n\nThe potential of convection then Monday will be modulated by the \nmovement of the storms earlier in the day, and any outflow \nboundaries that may be floating around. Areas that are not as much \nimpacted by any early morning storms will have a chance to \ndestabilize then with the heating of the day. This could lead to \nsome possible strong to severe storms with some better mid level \nflow helping with deep layer shear. The short wave is forecast to \nstay mainly north of the area, which would limit the severe \npotential some. We will continue to watch the trends with this.\n\n- Cooling off mid-week next week and drying out\n\nThere may be a lingering shower Tuesday morning with the upper \ntrough axis remaining nearby. This moves out on Tuesday, and then \nallows large scale subsidence and sfc ridging to build downstream of \nthe upper ridge over the Plains once again. This will be similar to \nthis week where we cool off under the NW flow, and remain dry. This \ndry period should go through next Wednesday. We will have to keep \nour eye out on the upper low that tries to cutoff with energy coming \nin from the NW and amplifying the upper flow. If it closes off \nearlier, it would mean better rain chances for our area.",
